3:["$","section",null,{"className":"mb-10","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"mb-4 text-2xl font-bold text-fg","children":"The Break-Even Formula in Plain English"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"leading-relaxed text-fg-muted mb-4","children":"The maths is straightforward: divide your CPC by your average order value and you get the conversion rate you need just to break even on ad spend alone - before fulfilment, overheads, or any margin."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"leading-relaxed text-fg-muted mb-4","children":["$","strong",null,{"className":"text-fg","children":"Break-even CVR = CPC ÷ AOV"}]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"leading-relaxed text-fg-muted mb-4","children":"At $50 CPC and $200 AOV: 50 ÷ 200 = 0.25, or 25%. You need one in four visitors to purchase just to recover your ad spend. Median ecommerce CVR is 1–3%. A well-optimised landing page might reach 5–8%. 25% is not a page optimisation problem - it is a unit economics mismatch. The CPC is too high for the AOV."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"leading-relaxed text-fg-muted mb-4","children":"That leaves three levers: bring the CPC down (tighter targeting, better Quality Score, different channels), bring the AOV up (bundles, upsells, higher-tier positioning), or accept the first-purchase loss and justify it through lifetime value. Everything else is rearranging deck chairs."}]]}]
4:["$","section",null,{"className":"mb-10","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"mb-4 text-2xl font-bold text-fg","children":"What Different CVR Thresholds Actually Look Like on a Page"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"leading-relaxed text-fg-muted mb-4","children":"CVR is not random. Pages that convert at 1%, 3%, and 8% are structurally different - not cosmetically different. Understanding what separates them tells you where your own page sits."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"leading-relaxed text-fg-muted mb-4","children":[["$","strong",null,{"className":"text-fg","children":"1% CVR pages"}]," typically have a generic headline, product-first copy (features before outcomes), a single CTA buried below the fold, little or no social proof, and no objection handling. They look like a brochure. Visitors arrive, feel uncertain, and leave."]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"leading-relaxed text-fg-muted mb-4","children":[["$","strong",null,{"className":"text-fg","children":"3% CVR pages"}]," lead with a specific outcome for a specific person, have a clear above-fold CTA, and include at least one trust signal - a testimonial, a logo bar, a guarantee. The copy addresses the primary objection. There is friction reduction (clear returns policy, visible pricing, no surprises at checkout). These pages convert the already-willing."]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"leading-relaxed text-fg-muted mb-4","children":[["$","strong",null,{"className":"text-fg","children":"8% CVR pages"}]," do something different altogether: they convert the not-yet-willing. The headline names the problem the visitor just typed into Google. The page structure moves from pain → credibility → mechanism → proof → offer → risk removal. There are multiple CTAs calibrated to different buying stages, urgency is contextual not manufactured, and the copy pre-empts the top three reasons someone might not buy. These pages don't just capture intent - they create it."]}]]}]
5:["$","section",null,{"className":"mb-10","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"mb-4 text-2xl font-bold text-fg","children":"The LTV Adjustment: When Losing on the First Purchase Is the Strategy"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"leading-relaxed text-fg-muted mb-4","children":"The break-even formula above assumes you only ever sell once. For SaaS with monthly recurring revenue, repeat ecommerce, or high-ticket service businesses with strong referral loops, that assumption is wrong - and acting on it will make you underinvest in acquisition."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"leading-relaxed text-fg-muted mb-4","children":"If a customer pays $200 upfront but generates $800 in lifetime revenue, your real break-even CVR is 50 ÷ 800 = 6.25%, not 25%. That's a number a well-built page can actually hit. The constraint isn't the CPC - it's whether your LTV data is reliable enough to justify the initial loss."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"leading-relaxed text-fg-muted mb-4","children":"When the page is asking for a loss-leader action, it needs to do something different: it must sell the relationship, not just the transaction. That means demonstrating what ongoing value looks like - retention proof (customer tenure, usage stats, case studies spanning multiple months), not just first-purchase testimonials. The page is recruiting a long-term customer, not closing a one-time sale, and the copy must reflect that."}]]}]
6:["$","section",null,{"className":"mb-10","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"mb-4 text-2xl font-bold text-fg","children":"Page Quality Is a Unit Economics Lever, Not a Branding Decision"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"leading-relaxed text-fg-muted mb-4","children":"Here is the reframe that changes how founders think about their pages: every percentage point of CVR improvement has a direct dollar value. It is not subjective. It is arithmetic."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"leading-relaxed text-fg-muted mb-4","children":["$","strong",null,{"className":"text-fg","children":"Monthly revenue gain from CVR lift = (traffic × CVR lift) × AOV"}]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"leading-relaxed text-fg-muted mb-4","children":"At 1,000 visitors per month, $200 AOV, improving CVR from 2% to 3% adds 10 sales: $2,000 per month, $24,000 per year. That same lift simultaneously reduces your effective CPA - meaning your existing ad budget suddenly performs 50% better without spending an extra dollar on clicks."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"leading-relaxed text-fg-muted mb-4","children":"A page redesign or CRO project that costs $3,000 and delivers a 1 percentage point CVR lift at that traffic volume pays for itself in six weeks. No paid media investment offers that return profile. The founders who understand this stop treating their landing page as a creative asset and start treating it as the highest-leverage financial instrument in their acquisition stack."}]]}]
7:["$","section",null,{"className":"mb-10","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"mb-4 text-2xl font-bold text-fg","children":"The Dangerous Middle: Pages That Look Professional but Guarantee Losses"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"leading-relaxed text-fg-muted mb-4","children":"The worst position in paid traffic is a page converting at 0.5% that looks polished enough to keep running. It doesn't trigger alarm bells. The creative looks fine. The layout is clean. There are no obvious errors. So the ads keep spending, the data keeps accumulating, and the losses compound quietly in the background."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"leading-relaxed text-fg-muted mb-4","children":"These pages fail for structural reasons, not aesthetic ones. The headline speaks to the product rather than the visitor's problem. The above-fold section answers the wrong question. The trust signals are there but they're the wrong type - brand logos when the visitor needs peer testimonials, or awards when they need a guarantee. The CTA asks for too much too soon from a cold audience."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"leading-relaxed text-fg-muted mb-4","children":"The danger is that a 0.5% CVR page is good enough to make you think the channel doesn't work, when actually the channel is fine and the page is the constraint. Founders kill profitable ad channels every week because they attribute the poor return to the traffic source rather than the landing page. The page is the last thing they change, when it should have been the first."}]]}]
8:["$","section",null,{"className":"mb-12 rounded-2xl border border-accent/20 bg-accent/5 px-8 py-10","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"mb-3 text-xl font-bold text-fg","children":"Know What Your Page Is Actually Delivering Before You Touch Your Bids"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"leading-relaxed text-fg-muted mb-6","children":"Before optimising your bids, know what your page is currently delivering. The free audit shows the structural elements - headline, CTA, trust signals, above-fold structure - that most often limit CVR. You'll see exactly where your page sits on the 1% / 3% / 8% spectrum, and what specific changes would move it."}],["$","$L2",null,{"href":"/audit?utm_source=learning-centre&utm_medium=organic-content","className":"inline-block rounded-lg bg-accent px-6 py-3 text-sm font-semibold text-white transition-opacity hover:opacity-90","children":"Get the free landing page audit →"}]]}]
9:["$","section",null,{"children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"mb-5 text-lg font-bold text-fg","children":"Related articles"}],["$","ul",null,{"className":"space-y-3","children":[["$","li",null,{"children":["$","$L2",null,{"href":"/learning-centre/high-cpc-low-conversion","className":"text-accent underline-offset-4 hover:underline","children":"High CPC, Low Conversion: Why Your Traffic Quality Isn't the Problem"}]}],["$","li",null,{"children":["$","$L2",null,{"href":"/learning-centre/before-you-raise-ad-budget","className":"text-accent underline-offset-4 hover:underline","children":"Before You Raise Your Ad Budget, Check These Five Page Metrics"}]}],["$","li",null,{"children":["$","$L2",null,{"href":"/learning-centre/landing-page-conversion-rate-benchmark","className":"text-accent underline-offset-4 hover:underline","children":"Landing Page Conversion Rate Benchmarks by Industry and Traffic Source"}]}],["$","li",null,{"children":["$","$L2",null,{"href":"/learning-centre/paid-traffic-leak-map","className":"text-accent underline-offset-4 hover:underline","children":"The Paid Traffic Leak Map: Where Your Ad Spend Is Actually Going"}]}]]}]]}]
